//! fails to build when the function is missing or its signature has moved. Every type is fully
//! qualified (`ref_id::Foo`) on purpose — a name that does not exist in the crate is exactly the kind
//! of failure this file exists to surface, self-contained to the one binding that names it.
//!
//! A parameter typed `IdentifierOrParsed` in the specification gets two bindings for the same method —
//! one with every such parameter as `&str`, one with every such parameter as `&ref_id::ParseResult` —
//! because `IdentifierOrParsed` is not itself a Rust type and today's functions are not generic over
//! the two; one binding of such a pair is expected to fail until the function is made generic.
#![allow(dead_code, non_upper_case_globals)]

